  "notInTheCatalogue": {
    "worked.paired": "ASKED FOR AND DOES NOT EXIST. The catalogue has `worked.single` and `visual.compare`; there is no `worked.paired`. If two worked examples side by side is wanted as its own pattern it would be a 6/6 of two `worked` slots — the same legitimate pairing as visual.compare's cases — but it is not designed, so this atlas cannot render it. It needs a decision, not a guess."
  },
  "edgeRule": "A PATTERN HAS ONE LEFT EDGE OR ONE CENTRE LINE, NEVER BOTH. Found by looking at the first boards: interactive.primary put its intro at 8/4 (starting at column 1) and its instrument at centred-8 (starting at column 3), so the page had two left edges and neither looked deliberate. Each pattern now declares `edge`: a `left-edge` pattern starts every prose row at column 1 and may still CENTRE ITS MEDIA, because a centred object on a left-aligned page reads as a deliberate stage; a `spine` pattern centres everything on one line. A control refuses a pattern that mixes them.",
  "unreachable": "APPROVED AND UNREACHABLE, named rather than quietly removed. Nine rows of these tables are for geometry classes no fixture can supply to that pattern's slot: `notes.illustration` admits only images and there is no WIDE image; `interactive.primary.instrument` admits only interactives and there is neither a PORTRAIT nor a WIDE one. Leaving the rows in means the table is complete if such an object is ever authored; the atlas prints the list on every run so the gap is visible rather than implied. It is a gap in the fixture set, not a renderer ignoring its table, and control 6 is scoped to the classes a run actually rendered so it does not confuse the two."
}
